./data/fulltext_screening_context_2026-07-10_docling_graph_direct_all235_clean_both_targets/fulltext_screening_input.jsonl:31:{"record_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_000626", "candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_000626", "source_record_id": "rec_000626", "source_corpus": "include", "title": "Probing genomic language models: Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer and the role of pretraining in learned representations", "abstract": "The value and nature of the representations learned during the pretraining of genomic language models (gLMs) remain actively debated. We introduce Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer (GPT), a decoder-only transformer with single-nucleotide tokenization, to dissect the role of pretraining. Through experiments varying repetitive element (RE) weights during pretraining (0.0 -1.0), comparative finetuning against random initialization, linear probing of internal representations, and sparse autoencoder (SAE)-based interpretability, we evaluated the impact of pretraining and how REs in genomic data influence model learning. Models with moderate RE downweighting (0.5) consistently achieved optimal performance across seven genomic classification tasks, with pretrained models providing substantial performance gains over baselines. SAE feature annotation via sequence alignment revealed substantial RE-associated patterns in the pretrained model internal representations, suggesting that REs -which comprise 30% -60% of mammalian genomes -may dominate the pretraining objective. Our findings support the utility of pretraining and underscore the need for pretraining strategies that better accommodate repetitive sequences across the genome while also fostering the learning of less common but biologically important representations. This study highlights a key challenge for gLMs: ensuring that models broadly learn functional genomic syntax beyond simply recognizing ubiquitous repeats. [truncated]", "doi": "10.1093/bib/bbag011", "year": "", "venue": "", "sources": [], "selected_full_text_sections": "[data_source: Probing genomic language models: Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer and the role of pretraining in learned representations > Methods > Datasets]\nFor pretraining, Nucleotide GPT was trained on the human reference genome (GRCh38), comprising ∼ 3.1 billion nucleotides. The genome was divided into non-overlapping 8192 bp sequences for model input. REs in the genome were identified using RepeatMasker annotations to enable weighted loss experiments.\n\nWe evaluated Nucleotide GPT on seven benchmark datasets from the Genomic Benchmarks collection (accessible via the Genomic Benchmarks repository bmcgenomdata.biomedcentral.com): (i) Coding Versus Intergenic-binary classification of protein-coding versus intergenic sequences, (ii) Ensembl Regulatory-multi-class classification of promoters, enhancers, and OCRs, (iii) Non-TATA Promoters-binary classification of human non-TATA promoter sequences, (iv) Ensembl Enhancers-binary classification of enhancer sequences, (v) Cohn Enhancers-binary classification of enhancer sequences from an alternative dataset, (vi) Ensembl OCRs-binary classification of OCRs, and (vii) Human or worm-species classification between human and Caenorhabditis elegans sequences. Each dataset is supplied with predefined training and test splits. All input sequences c onsist of standard DNA nucleotides (A, C, G, T).\n\nThe datasets used for fi netuning are derived entirely from genomic sequences, with both positive and negative samples extracted from reference genomes. For human datasets, positive samples represent annotated functional elements (promoters, enhancers, open chromatin regions) from databases such as Ensembl Regulatory Build, FANTOM5, and EPD. Negative samples were randomly generated from the same reference genome (GRCh38) to match the length distribution of positive samples while avoiding overlap with annotated elements. Since our model was pretrained on the GRCh38 human reference genome, there is inherent overlap: all benchmark sequences (both positive and negative) are subsequences of the pretraining data. However, this does not constitute data leakage for several reasons: (i) pretraining used unsupervised causal language modeling on continuous 8192 bp windows without any functional labels, (ii) finetuning uses much shorter sequences with supervised labels, and (iii) the model must learn to distinguish functional elements from genomic background based on sequence patterns alone, not memorized position-label associations. This setup mirrors the intended use case where models pretrained on reference genomes are fine-tuned to identify functional elements within those same genomes.\n\n[input_representation: Probing genomic language models: Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer and the role of pretraining in learned representations > Results]\nNucleotide GPT employs the characteristic gLM two-stage training paradigm of pretraining and fi netuning to evaluate the role and nature of learned representations (Fig. 1A). Architecturally, Nucleotide GPT is inspired by the LLaMA-style decoder-only transformer design, adopting several key components shown to improve training stability and performance in NLP: (i) applying Root Mean Square Normalization (RMS) before the attention and feed-forward blocks within each layer, instead of the traditional post-layer normalization, and (ii) employing the Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE) for positional encoding (Fig. 1B) [17-19]. The model comprises 12 such transformer la yers with a model dimension of 2048.\n\nA crucial design choice is its single-nucleotide tokenization, where each base is treated as an individual token, preserving the highest sequence resolution. The initial pretraining stage aims to develop broad genomic sequence understanding by training the model on the human reference genome (GRCh38) using a self-supervised CLM objective-predicting the next nucleotide in 8192 bp sequences. In the subsequent fi netuning stage, these learned representations are refined for genomic understanding classification tasks, requiring minimal architectural modifications to the base model.\n\nModels were evaluated using the Genomic Benchmarks collection [20]. We fi rst evaluated the models on a binary classification task distinguishing coding from intergenic sequences. The mammalian genome can be broadly separated into coding (sequences related to protein coding genes) and intergenic (sequences that do not code for protein and lie between coding genes). Coding genes account for ∼ 2% of the genome, and intergenic regions contain a broad array of other elements, such as regulatory sequences and genes for noncoding RNAs. To evaluate models' ability to classify regulatory elements, we use a multi-class dataset of human regulatory sequences derived from the Ensembl database, in addition to binary tasks classifying each type of regulatory sequence individually. These tasks focus on discriminating between three key categories involved in transcriptional control: promoters, which are sequences typically found upstream of gene start sites that serve as the binding site for RNA polymerase and initiate transcription; enhancers, which act as regulatory 'switches' that can be located far from the gene they influence but significantly boost its expression level by interacting with the promoter machinery; and open chromatin regions (OCRs). Open chromatin does not represent a single element type but rather signifies areas where the DNA is physically accessible to regulatory proteins because the surrounding chromatin structure is relaxed. While functionally related, accurately distinguishing promoters, enhancers, and the OCRs based on their underlying sequence features is crucial for understanding the logic on gene regulation. Models were also evaluated on classifying nonTATA promoter sequences, regulatory regions that initiate gene transcription despite lacking the canonical TATA-box sequence motif, often relying on other elements like Initiator (Inr) or Downstream Promoter Element (DPE) sequences [21].\n\n#### Downweighting of REs optimizes performance\n\nGiven that REs comprise 30%-60% of mammalian genomes, we fi rst investigated how their treatment during pretraining affects downstream performance. We trained four variants of Nucleotide GPT with different loss weights applied to tokens identified as REs: 0.0 (complete exclusion), 0.1 (90% downweighting), 0.5 (50% downweighting), and 1.0 (no downweighting) (Table 1). Across seven genomic classification tasks, the model with 0.5 weighting consistently achieved optimal or near-optimal performance.\n\n#### Pretraining provides substantial performance gains over baselines\n\nTo evaluate the value of pretraining with optimized RE handling, we compared the 0.5-weighted pretrained model against two baselines: a simple linear classifier on one-hot encoded sequences and the\n\n|\n\nFigure 1 Overview of nucleotide GPT training workflow and architecture. (A) the training pipeline for training task-specific fi netuned models. A foundational gLM is fi rst pretrained with CLM on the human genome. This pretrained model is then adapted through supervised fi netuning using smaller, annotated datasets tailored to specific downstream biological tasks, resulting in multiple specialized fi netuned gLMs. (B) the architecture of Nucleotide GPT. Input DNA sequences are processed as individual tokens into an input embedding layer. Positional information is incorporated using RoPE. The core of the model consists of 12 stacked transformer blocks. Each block utilizes pre- and post-layer RMS normalization to the multi-head attention block (configured with 8 heads and a key dimension of 128) and feed-forward network with GeLU activation. Residual connections bypass these sub-layers. The fi nal output is generated after passing through an output layer, a fi nal RMS normalization step, and an output projection layer. Figure made with BioRender.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nTable 1 Effect of RE loss weighting on nucleotide GPT performance across genomic benchmark classification tasks.\n\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n\n| Dataset . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   | 0.0                   | 0.0                  | 0.0                       | 0.1                   | 0.1                  | 0.1                       | 0.5                   | 0.5                | 0.5                     | 1.0                   | 1.0                | 1.0                   |\n|-------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------|----------------------|---------------------------|-----------------------|----------------------|---------------------------|-----------------------|--------------------|-------------------------|-----------------------|--------------------|-----------------------|\n| Dataset . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   | ACC . . . . . . . . . | F1 . . . . . . . . . | MCC . . . . . . . . . . . | ACC . . . . . . . . . | F1 . . . . . . . . . | MCC . . . . . . . . . . . | ACC . . . . . . . . . | F1 . . . . . . . . | MCC . . . . . . . . . . | ACC . . . . . . . . . | F1 . . . . . . . . | MCC . . . . . . . . . |\n| Coding versus intergenic                              | 92.4                  | 92.0                 | 85.0                      | 92.0          