   | 95.9                  | 95.9                 | 91.72                     | 95.3                  | 95.4                 | 90.7                      | 96.5                  | 96.5               | 93.0                    | 96.3                  | 96.3               | 92.6                  |\n\nBold values indicate the highest scoring model; underlined values indicate the second h ighest.\n\nsame Nucleotide GPT architecture initialized with random weights ( Table 2). The pretrained model consistently outperformed both baselines across all seven tasks, with accuracy improvements ranging from 1.0% to 33.8% over the linear baseline and 1.0%-15.9% over r andom initialization.\n\n#### Single-nucleotide tokenization outperforms k-mer approaches\n\nWe compared single-nucleotide tokenization against two k-mer tokenization strategies: overlapping and non-overlapping 6-mers\n\n(Table 3). Single-nucleotide tokenization achieved the highest performance on most tasks, with particularly large advantages for nonTATA promoters (94.5% versus 88.4% for overlapping 6-mers and 89.0% for non-overlapping) and Ensembl OCRs (77.1% versus 75.1% and 73.3%). The overlapping 6-mer approach showed competitive performance on the Ensembl Regulatory Sequences task (93.6% versus 93.2%), suggesting that for some multi-class regulatory classification problems, capturing fixed-length sequence patterns may provide slight benefits. However, single-nucleotide tokenization's consistent strong performance across diverse tasks, combined with its preservation of biological resolution, validates this design choice.\n\n|\n\nTable 2 Comparison of Pretrained nucleotide GPT against baseline models across genomic benchmark classification tasks.\n\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .