. . . . | F1 . . . . . . | MCC . . . . . . . |\n| Coding versus intergenic                                      | 90.0          | 90.0         | 80.0            | 90.5            | 90.5           | 81.0              | 84.2            | 84.1           | 68.4              |\n| Ensembl regulatory                                            | 64.5          | 64.3         | 46.5            | 67.2            | 67.2           | 50.6              | 76.6            | 76.7           | 76.9              |\n| Nontata promoters                                             | 86.8          | 87.6         | 73.5            | 86.9            | 87.7           | 73.9              | 81.6            | 82.5           | 63.4              |\n| Ensembl enhancers                                             | 79.7          | 80.0         | 59.3            | 81.3            | 81.4           | 62.6              | 77.0            | 77.3           | 54.0              |\n| Cohn enhancers                                                | 71.9          | 72.1         | 43.8            | 71.9            | 71.8           | 43.7              | 70.4            | 70.4           | 40.8              |\n| Ensembl OCRs                                                  | 70.6          | 71.0         | 41.3            | 73.6            | 73.7           | 47.2              | 67.8            | 68.7           | 35.6              |\n| Human or worm                                                 | 95.6          | 95.6         | 91.1            | 95.9            | 96.0           | 91.9              | 93.3            | 93.3           | 86.5              |\n\nBold values indicate the highest scoring model; underlined values indicate the second h ighest.\n\nTo assess whether different layers learn distinct versus redundant representations, we computed Pearson correlations between linear probe predictions from all layer pairs for pretrained, fi netuned, and random initialization models (Fig. 3). Pretrained and fi netuned models exhibited nearly identical hierarchical block structures across all tasks. Early layers showed low prediction correlations with later layers while predictions within each block were highly correlated ( r &gt; .95 among layers 5-12) The transition point around layer 4 corresponds precisely to where probe accuracy plateaus, suggesting that layers 14 progressively build task-relevant representations, while layers 5-12 maintain these compressed representations with minimal additional transformation. The similarity between pretrained and fi netuned correlation structures indicates that task-specific fi netuning preserves the hierarchical organization established during pretraining rather than fundamentally reorganizing it. In contrast, randomly initialized models showed uniformly high correlations ( r &gt; .90) across nearly all layer pairs, with minimal block structure. Combined with the fl at probe accuracy profiles, this suggests random initialization leads to redundant rather than hierarchical processing across depth.\n\n#### SAE features are enriched for REs\n\nAfter pretraining the 0.5-weighted Nucleotide GPT, we trained an SAE on the intermediate activations of the model's residual stream. The SAE expanded the 2048-dimensional activations to a sparse 8192-dimensional latent space and then reconstructed the original representation. Linear probes trained on these sparse SAE features achieved compar able performance to probes trained on dense activations across classification tasks (Supplementary Table 1), suggesting that sparse decomposition may preserve task-relevant information. By extracting the top sequences that maximally activated each latent feature, we manually annotated f eatures using RE alignment tool Dfam [24]. REs are DNA sequences present in multiple copies that constitute 30-60% of mammalian genomes. Transposable elements (TEs), a major class of REs, are mobile DNA sequences that propagate within genomes [25]. While they can threaten genomic integrity if not properly regulated and may have deleterious effects, they can also represent a significant source of genetic variation that has contributed to evolutionary innovation [26].\n\nSeveral SAE features were notably enriched for TEs. Two latent features consistently responded to sequences with high similarity to the THE1D and THE1B repeats -elements belonging to the endogenous retrovirus-like mammalian-apparent long terminal repeat (LTR) retrotransposon (ERVL-MaLR) class (Table 6) [27]. Additional features highlighted distinct regions of Long Interspersed Nuclear Element (LINE) retrotransposons. One mapped primarily to the 3 ′ untranslated regions, which contain conserved polyadenylation signals and RNA motifs, while another concentrated on 5 ′ regions that harbor internal promoters for certain L1PA subfamilies (Table 6) [28]. Features also showed strong activation on sequences aligning to subfamilies of the highly abundant Alu family of Short Interspersed Nuclear Elements (SINEs) (Table 6). Alu elements are mobile DNA sequences comprising over 10% of the human genome and have been found to contribute significantly to genomic evolution through RNA editing, exonization, and transcript variation [29].\n\n#### REs show distinct enrichment patterns in regulatory r egions\n\nTo understand how REs are distributed across functional genomic regions, we performed enrichment analysis using the Genomic Association Tester (GAT) comparing 46 RE classes against promoters,\n\n|\n\nFigure 3 Layer-wise linear probe prediction correlations for genomic benchmarks classification tasks. Heatmaps showing Pearson correlation coefficients (R) between linear probe predictions from all 12 transformer layers. Each panel compares three training conditions: Pretrained model (left), fi netuned model (center), and random initialization fi netuned model (right). Linear probes were trained independently on 2048-dimensional globally-averaged activation vectors extracted from each of the 12 transformer layers.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n|\n\nTable 6 Dfam alignment results for maximally activating SAE features\n\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n\nFigure 4 RE class enrichment across human regulatory regions. Heatmap showing fold enrichment (observed/expected) of 15 RE classes across promoter regions ( + / -2 kb from transcription start sites, n = 61 775), enhancer regions (FANTOM5, n = 43 011), and OCRs (ENCODE DNase-seq peaks, n = 217 847) in the human genome (hg38). Colors represent log2-transformed fold enrichment values, with red indicating enrichment and blue indicating depletion relative to genomic backgr ound. Numerical values show actual fold enrichment with statistical significance after BenjaminiHochberg correction indicated by asterisks ( ∗∗∗ q &lt; .001, ∗ &lt; .05). Analysis performed using GAT with 20 000 permutations.\n\n|   Feature # . . . . . . . . . . . . | Repeat element . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   | Family . . . . . . . . . . . .   | Class . . . . . .   |   Bit score . . . . . . . . . . . |   E -value . . . . . . . . . . | Strand . . . . . . . . .   | Accession # . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   |\n|-------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|---------------------|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n|                                3598 | L1P1 orf2                                        | L1                               | LINE                |                              96.7 |                        2.8e-30 | -                          | DF000000316.4                                   |\n|                                 177 | L1PA2 3end                                       | L1                               | LINE                |                             105.9 |                          3e-32 | -                          | DF000000339.4                                   |\n|                                 907 | L1PA3 3end                                       | L1                               | LINE                |                             104.6 |                        8.1e-32 | -                          | DF000000340.4                                   |\n|                                 999 | L1M4 orf2                                        | L1                               | LINE                |                              48.3 |                        9.3e-16 | +                          | DF000000250.4                                   |\n|                                5015 | L1PA12 5end                                      | L1                               | LINE                |                              58.9 |                        5.5e-19 | -                          | DF000000778.4                                   |\n|                                 347 | THE1D                                            | ERVL-MaLR                        | LTR                 |                              83.5 |                        7.4e-25 | -                          | DF000000820.4                                   |\n|                                 997 | THE1B                                            | ERVL-MaLR                        | LTR                 |                              51.1 |                        4.4e-15 | -                          | DF000000818.4                                   |\n|                                5318 | AluSx1                                           | Alu                              | SINE                |                              97.8 |                        2.6e-29 | +                          | DF000000048.4                                   |\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nenhancers, and OCRs in the human genome ( Fig. 4). We observed highly significant enrichment and depletion patterns across different repeat classes and region types. Small RNA genes showed the strongest enrichment in promoter regions, with scRNA (8.74-fold), snRNA (6.85-fold), RNA (6.60-fold), srpRNA (6.31-fold), and rRNA (5.02-fold) all significantly enriched ( q &lt; .001). Notably, tRNA genes displayed extreme enrichment in OCRs (13.69-fold) while showing more modest enrichment in promoters and enhancers. Major TE classes - including SINE, LINE, LTR, and DNA transposons-exhibited progressive depletion from promoters to OCRs. LINE elements decreased from near-neutral in promoters (1.00-fold) to substantial depletion in OCRs (0.34-fold), while the retrotransposon class showed extreme depletion in accessible chromatin (0.05-fold). In contrast, simple repeats and low complexity sequences were consistently enriched across all three regulatory region types, with low complexity sequences reaching 2.79-fold enrichment in OCRs. These distinct enrichment and depletion patterns suggest that RE distributions serve as informative features for distinguishing regulatory regions. This may explain why models trained to recognize RE during pretraining show improved performance on regulatory classification tasks, as the presence or absence of specific repeat classes provides a robust signal for identifying different types of functional regions.", "section_evidence": [{"section_type": "data_source", "heading": "Datasets", "heading_path": ["Probing genomic language models: Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer and the role of pretraining in learned representations", "Methods", "Datasets"], "heading_path_text": "Probing genomic language models: Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer and the role of pretraining in learned representations > Methods > Datasets", "heading_level": 4, "line_start": 255, "line_end": 262, "text": "For pretraining, Nucleotide GPT was trained on the human reference genome (GRCh38), comprising ∼ 3.1 billion nucleotides. The genome was divided into non-overlapping 8192 bp sequences for model input. REs in the genome were identified using RepeatMasker annotations to enable weighted loss experiments.\n\nWe evaluated Nucleotide GPT on seven benchmark datasets from the Genomic Benchmarks collection (accessible via the Genomic Benchmarks repository bmcgenomdata.biomedcentral.com): (i) Coding Versus Intergenic-binary classification of protein-coding versus intergenic sequences, (ii) Ensembl Regulatory-multi-class classification of promoters, enhancers, and OCRs, (iii) Non-TATA Promoters-binary classification of human non-TATA promoter sequences, (iv) Ensembl Enhancers-binary classification of enhancer sequences, (v) Cohn Enhancers-binary classification of enhancer sequences from an alternative dataset, (vi) Ensembl OCRs-binary classification of OCRs, and (vii) Human or worm-species classification between human and Caenorhabditis elegans sequences. Each dataset is supplied with predefined training and test splits. All input sequences c onsist of standard DNA nucleotides (A, C, G, T).\n\nThe datasets used for fi netuning are derived entirely from genomic sequences, with both positive and negative samples extracted from reference genomes. For human datasets, positive samples represent annotated functional elements (promoters, enhancers, open chromatin regions) from databases such as Ensembl Regulatory Build, FANTOM5, and EPD. Negative samples were randomly generated from the same reference genome (GRCh38) to match the length distribution of positive samples while avoiding overlap with annotated elements. Since our model was pretrained on the GRCh38 human reference genome, there is inherent overlap: all benchmark sequences (both positive and negative) are subsequences of the pretraining data. However, this does not constitute data leakage for several reasons: (i) pretraining used unsupervised causal language modeling on continuous 8192 bp windows without any functional labels, (ii) finetuning uses much shorter sequences with supervised labels, and (iii) the model must learn to distinguish functional elements from genomic background based on sequence patterns alone, not memorized position-label associations. This setup mirrors the intended use case where models pretrained on reference genomes are fine-tuned to identify functional elements within those same genomes.", "original_chars": 2497, "evidence_quote": "Nucleotide GPT was trained on the human reference genome (GRCh38)", "evidence_section_heading": "Methods", "contains_evidence_quote": true, "contains_chunk_excerpt": true, "chunk_id": 44, "pages": [10], "doc_item_refs": ["#/texts/114", "#/texts/115", "#/texts/116"], "docling_graph_node_id": "DataSourceEvidence_5bb34aee0b0ce3b9", "docling_graph_provenance": {"document_id": "73c20120db4438b0a4ac2b1ce9aff4df", "match": "verbatim", "chunks": [4, 44], "pages": [2, 10], "refs": ["#/texts/27", "#/texts/28", "#/texts/29", "#/texts/114", "#/texts/115", "#/texts/116"], "spans": [{"chunk": 4, "start": 1123, "end": 1154}, {"chunk": 44, "start": 201, "end": 232}]}, "grounding_source": "docling_graph_provenance", "match_type": "verbatim", "source_markdown": "data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_000626.md", "derivation_source": "docling_markdown_heading_boundary_from_docling_graph_heading_path", "section_coverage_ratio": 0.032794, "root_container": false, "full_document_like": false, "target_section_types": ["data_source"]}, {"section_type": "input_representation", "heading": "Results", "heading_path": ["Probing genomic language models: Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer and the role of pretraining in learned representations", "Results"], "heading_path_text": "Probing genomic language models: Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer and the role of pretraining in learned representations > Results", "heading_level": 3, "line_start": 39, "line_end": 230, "text": "Nucleotide GPT employs the characteristic gLM two-stage training paradigm of pretraining and fi netuning to evaluate the role and nature of learned representations (Fig. 1A). Architecturally, Nucleotide GPT is inspired by the LLaMA-style decoder-only transformer design, adopting several key components shown to improve training stability and performance in NLP: (i) applying Root Mean Square Normalization (RMS) before the attention and feed-forward blocks within each layer, instead of the traditional post-layer normalization, and (ii) employing the Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE) for positional encoding (Fig. 1B) [17-19]. The model comprises 12 such transformer la yers with a model dimension of 2048.\n\nA crucial design choice is its single-nucleotide tokenization, where each base is treated as an individual token, preserving the highest sequence resolution. The initial pretraining stage aims to develop broad genomic sequence understanding by training the model on the human reference genome (GRCh38) using a self-supervised CLM objective-predicting the next nucleotide in 8192 bp sequences. In the subsequent fi netuning stage, these learned representations are refined for genomic understanding classification tasks, requiring minimal architectural modifications to the base model.\n\nModels were evaluated using the Genomic Benchmarks collection [20]. We fi rst evaluated the models on a binary classification task distinguishing coding from intergenic sequences. The mammalian genome can be broadly separated into coding (sequences related to protein coding genes) and intergenic (sequences that do not code for protein and lie between coding genes). Coding genes account for ∼ 2% of the genome, and intergenic regions contain a broad array of other elements, such as regulatory sequences and genes for noncoding RNAs. To evaluate models' ability to classify regulatory elements, we use a multi-class dataset of human regulatory sequences derived from the Ensembl database, in addition to binary tasks classifying each type of regulatory sequence individually. These tasks focus on discriminating between three key categories involved in transcriptional control: promoters, which are sequences typically found upstream of gene start sites that serve as the binding site for RNA polymerase and initiate transcription; enhancers, which act as regulatory 'switches' that can be located far from the gene they influence but significantly boost its expression level by interacting with the promoter machinery; and open chromatin regions (OCRs). Open chromatin does not represent a single element type but rather signifies areas where the DNA is physically accessible to regulatory proteins because the surrounding chromatin structure is relaxed. While functionally related, accurately distinguishing promoters, enhancers, and the OCRs based on their underlying sequence features is crucial for understanding the logic on gene regulation. Models were also evaluated on classifying nonTATA promoter sequences, regulatory regions that initiate gene transcription despite lacking the canonical TATA-box sequence motif, often relying on other elements like Initiator (Inr) or Downstream Promoter Element (DPE) sequences [21].\n\n#### Downweighting of REs optimizes performance\n\nGiven that REs comprise 30%-60% of mammalian genomes, we fi rst investigated how their treatment during pretraining affects downstream performance. We trained four variants of Nucleotide GPT with different loss weights applied to tokens identified as REs: 0.0 (complete exclusion), 0.1 (90% downweighting), 0.5 (50% downweighting), and 1.0 (no downweighting) (Table 1). Across seven genomic classification tasks, the model with 0.5 weighting consistently achieved optimal or near-optimal performance.\n\n#### Pretraining provides substantial performance gains over baselines\n\nTo evaluate the value of pretraining with optimized RE handling, we compared the 0.5-weighted pretrained model against two baselines: a simple linear classifier on one-hot encoded sequences and the\n\n|\n\nFigure 1 Overview of nucleotide GPT training workflow and architecture. (A) the training pipeline for training task-specific fi netuned models. A foundational gLM is fi rst pretrained with CLM on the human genome. This pretrained model is then adapted through supervised fi netuning using smaller, annotated datasets tailored to specific downstream biological tasks, resulting in multiple specialized fi netuned gLMs. (B) the architecture of Nucleotide GPT. Input DNA sequences are processed as individual tokens into an input embedding layer. Positional information is incorporated using RoPE. The core of the model consists of 12 stacked transformer blocks. Each block utilizes pre- and post-layer RMS normalization to the multi-head attention block (configured with 8 heads and a key dimension of 128) and feed-forward network with GeLU activation. Residual connections bypass these sub-layers. The fi nal output is generated after passing through an output layer, a fi nal RMS normalization step, and an output projection layer. Figure made with BioRender.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nTable 1 Effect of RE loss weighting on nucleotide GPT performance across genomic benchmark classification tasks.\n\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .