             | 90.7                  | 81.23                 | 91.8                      | 91.7                      | 83.7                      | 92.4                | 92.6                | 84.9                |\n| Ensembl regulatory                                            | 93.6                  | 93.6                  | 90.4                  | 91.7                      | 91.6                      | 87.8                      | 93.2                | 93.2                | 89.9                |\n| Nontata promoters                                             | 88.4                  | 89.1                  | 76.7                  | 89.0                      | 89.6                      | 78.2                      | 94.5                | 94.9                | 88.9                |\n| Ensembl enhancers                                             | 84.5                  | 69.5                  | 83.5                  | 82.7                      | 81.1                      | 66.2                      | 85.8                | 84.6                | 72.5                |\n| Cohn enhancers                                                | 72.1                  | 71.0                  | 44.2                  | 72.6                      | 75.2                      | 46.2                      | 73.2                | 72.4                | 46.5                |\n| Ensembl OCRs                                                  | 75.1                  | 76.1                  | 50.3                  | 73.3                      | 71.9                      | 46.7                      | 77.1                | 77.1                | 54.3                |\n| Human or worm                                                 | 95.6                  | 95.6                  | 91.2                  | 95.9                      | 95.9                      | 91.8                      | 96.5                | 96.5                | 93.0                |\n\nBold values indicate the highest scoring model; underlined values indicate the second highest.\n\n#### Nucleotide GPT achieves competitive performance against state-of-the-art models\n\nWhenbenchmarkedagainstpublishedstate-of-the-art gLMs, Nucleotide GPT demonstrated strong performance, achieving the highest accuracy on four out of seven tasks (Table 4). The comparison included DNABERT [3], which uses k-mer tokenization and MLM; and HyenaDNA [22] with 32 K context length, which employs long-r ange sequence modeling; and Nucleotide Transformer [4], which combines single-nucleotide tokenization with bidirectional attention. To ensure fair comparison, all baseline models were fi netuned on each task for one epoch using consistent hyperparameters (AdamW optimizer, learning rate of 3e-5 for DNABERT and Nucleotide Transformer, 1e4 f or HyenaDNA), matching the fi netuning approach for Nucleotide GPT.\n\n#### Linear probing reveals strong representations in Pretrained models\n\nTo further assess representation quality, we applied linear probes (logistic regression) to middle layer activations under three conditions: the base pretrained model, the model fi netuned after pretraining, and the model fi netuned from random initialization (Table 5). For most tasks, the pretrained model's representations showed strong linear separability even before fi netuning. For example, on Coding versus Intergenic classification, the pretrained model achieved 90.0% probe accuracy, nearly matching the fi netuned model (90.5%) and substantially exceeding the randomly initialized model (84.2%). Similarly, for non-TATA promoters, the pretrained (86.8%) and fi netuned (86.9%) models performed comparably, both outperforming random initialization (81.6%) by over 5%. The Ensembl Regulatory multi-class task was the exception to this trend, with the randomly initialized model's probe (76.6%) outperforming both pretrained (64.5%) and finetuned (67.2%) models, suggesting that task-specific fi netuning can reorganize representations for better linear separability even from r andom initialization, though the full model performance remained superior with pretraining (Table 2).\n\nTo understand how representations develop across model depth, we extended our linear probing analysis to all 12 transformer layers for four representative tasks: Coding versus Intergenic, Non-TATA Promoters, Human or Worm, and Ensembl Enhancers (Fig. 2). Since our probes extract activations from the residual stream at each la yer, we can track how task-relevant information accumulates through the network.\n\nAcross all four tasks, the pretrained and fi netuned models exhibited a consistent pattern: probe accuracy rose sharply in early layers (layers 1-4), then plateaued, with minimal further improvement in deeper layers. This early convergence pattern aligns with the information bottleneck principle: the network rapidly compresses task-relevant information in early-to-middle la yers, and subsequent layers maintain rather than substantially refine these compressed representations [23].\n\nRandomly initialized models showed fundamentally different dynamics. These models achieved moderately high probe accuracy in the earliest layers and immediately plateaued with no meaningful improvement across the remaining layers. While random models started with higher early-layer performance, pretrained and finetuned models surpassed them by layer 4 and maintained this advantage through all deeper layers. This pattern suggests the residual stream of randomly initialized models accumulates information that plateaus early rather than undergoing the compression and abstraction process characteristic of pretrained networks.\n\n|\n\nFigure 2 Layer-wise linear probe performance on genomic benchmarks classification tasks. Performance metrics (accuracy %, F1 score %, and Matthewscorrelation coefficient %) comparing linear probes trained independently on 2048-dimensional globally-averaged activation vectors extracted from each of the 12 transformer layers for thr e