5             | 96.5             | 93.0              |\n\nBold values indicate the highest scoring model; underlined values indicate the second h ighest.\n\nTable 5 Comparison of linear probe performance of Nucleotide GPT middle layer activations across genomic benchmark classification tasks.\n\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n\n|                                                               | Pretrained    | Pretrained   | Pretrained      | Finetuned       | Finetuned      | Finetuned         | Random          | Random         | Random            |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|--------------|-----------------|-----------------|----------------|-------------------|-----------------|----------------|-------------------|\n| Dataset . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . | ACC . . . . . | F1 . . . . . | MCC . . . . . . | ACC . . . . . . | F1 . . . . . . | MCC . . . . . . . | ACC . . . . . . | F1 . . . . . . | MCC . . . . . . . |\n| Coding versus intergenic                                      | 90.0          | 90.0         | 80.0            | 90.5            | 90.5           | 81.0              | 84.2            | 84.1           | 68.4              |\n| Ensembl regulatory                                            | 64.5          | 64.3         | 46.5            | 67.2            | 67.2           | 50.6              | 76.6            | 76.7           | 76.9              |\n| Nontata promoters                                             | 86.8          | 87.6         | 73.5            | 86.9            | 87.7           | 73.9              | 81.6            | 82.5           | 63.4              |\n| Ensembl enhancers                                             | 79.7          | 80.0         | 59.3            | 81.3            | 81.4           | 62.6              | 77.0            | 77.3           | 54.0              |\n| Cohn enhancers                                                | 71.9          | 72.1         | 43.8            | 71.9            | 71.8           | 43.7              | 70.4            | 70.4           | 40.8              |\n| Ensembl OCRs                                                  | 70.6          | 71.0         | 41.3            | 73.6            | 73.7           | 47.2              | 67.8            | 68.7           | 35.6              |\n| Human or worm                                                 | 95.6          | 95.6         | 91.1            | 95.9            | 96.0           | 91.9              | 93.3            | 93.3           | 86.5              |\n\nBold values indicate the highest scoring model; underlined values indicate the second h ighest.\n\nTo assess whether different layers learn distinct versus redundant representations, we computed Pearson correlations between linear probe predictions from all layer pairs for pretrained, fi netuned, and random initialization models (Fig. 3). Pretrained and fi netuned models exhibited nearly identical hierarchical block structures across all tasks. Early layers showed low prediction correlations with later layers while predictions within each block were highly correlated ( r &gt; .95 among layers 5-12) The transition point around layer 4 corresponds precisely to where probe accuracy plateaus, suggesting that layers 14 progressively build task-relevant representations, while layers 5-12 maintain these compressed representations with minimal additional transformation. The similarity between pretrained and fi netuned correlation structures indicates that task-specific fi netuning preserves the hierarchical organization established during pretraining rather than fundamentally reorganizing it. In contrast, randomly initialized models showed uniformly high correlations ( r &gt; .90) across nearly all layer pairs, with minimal block structure. Combined with the fl at probe accuracy profiles, this suggests random initialization leads to redundant rather than hierarchical processing across depth.\n\n#### SAE features are enriched for REs\n\nAfter pretraining the 0.5-weighted Nucleotide GPT, we trained an SAE on the intermediate activations of the model's residual stream. The SAE expanded the 2048-dimensional activations to a sparse 8192-dimensional latent space and then reconstructed the original representation. Linear probes trained on these sparse SAE features achieved compar able performance to probes trained on dense activations across classification tasks (Supplementary Table 1), suggesting that sparse decomposition may preserve task-relevant information. By extracting the top sequences that maximally activated each latent feature, we manually annotated f eatures using RE alignment tool Dfam [24]. REs are DNA sequences present in multiple copies that constitute 30-60% of mammalian genomes. Transposable elements (TEs), a major class of REs, are mobile DNA sequences that propagate within genomes [25]. While they can threaten genomic integrity if not properly regulated and may have deleterious effects, they can also represent a significant source of genetic variation that has contributed to evolutionary innovation [26].\n\nSeveral SAE features were notably enriched for TEs. Two latent features consistently responded to sequences with high similarity to the THE1D and THE1B repeats -elements belonging to the endogenous retrovirus-like mammalian-apparent long terminal repeat (LTR) retrotransposon (ERVL-MaLR) class (Table 6) [27]. Additional features highlighted distinct regions of Long Interspersed Nuclear Element (LINE) retrotransposons. One mapped primarily to the 3 ′ untranslated regions, which contain conserved polyadenylation signals and RNA motifs, while another concentrated on 5 ′ regions that harbor internal promoters for certain L1PA subfamilies (Table 6) [28]. Features also showed strong activation on sequences aligning to subfamilies of the highly abundant Alu family of Short Interspersed Nuclear Elements (SINEs) (Table 6). Alu elements are mobile DNA sequences comprising over 10% of the human genome and have been found to contribute significantly to genomic evolution through RNA editing, exonization, and transcript variation [29].\n\n#### REs show distinct enrichment patterns in regulatory r egions\n\nTo understand how REs are distributed across functional genomic regions, we performed enrichment analysis using the Genomic Association Tester (GAT) comparing 46 RE classes against promoters,\n\n|\n\nFigure 3 Layer-wise linear probe prediction correlations for genomic benchmarks classification tasks. Heatmaps showing Pearson correlation coefficients (R) between linear probe predictions from all 12 transformer layers. Each panel compares three training conditions: Pretrained model (left), fi netuned model (center), and random initialization fi netuned model (right). Linear probes were trained independently on 2048-dimensional globally-averaged activation vectors extracted from each of the 12 transformer layers.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n|\n\nTable 6 Dfam alignment results for maximally activating SAE features\n\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n\nFigure 4 RE class enrichment across human regulatory regions. Heatmap showing fold enrichment (observed/expected) of 15 RE classes across promoter regions ( + / -2 kb from transcription start sites, n = 61 775), enhancer regions (FANTOM5, n = 43 011), and OCRs (ENCODE DNase-seq peaks, n = 217 847) in the human genome (hg38). Colors represent log2-transformed fold enrichment values, with red indicating enrichment and blue indicating depletion relative to genomic backgr ound. Numerical values show actual fold enrichment with statistical significance after BenjaminiHochberg correction indicated by asterisks ( ∗∗∗ q &lt; .001, ∗ &lt; .05). Analysis performed using GAT with 20 000 permutations.\n\n|   Feature # . . . . . . . . . . . . | Repeat element . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   | Family . . . . . . . . . . . .   | Class . . . . . .   |   Bit score . . . . . . . . . . . |   E -value . . . . . . . . . . | Strand . . . . . . . . .   | Accession # . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   |\n|-------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|---------------------|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n|                                3598 | L1P1 orf2                                        | L1                               | LINE                |                              96.7 |                        2.8e-30 | -                          | DF000000316.4                                   |\n|                                 177 | L1PA2 3end                                       | L1                               | LINE                |                             105.9 |                          3e-32 | -                          | DF000000339.4                                   |\n|                                 907 | L1PA3 3end                                       | L1                               | LINE                |                             104.6 |                        8.1e-32 | -                          | DF000000340.4                                   |\n|                                 999 | L1M4 orf2                                        | L1                               | LINE                |                              48.3 |                        9.3e-16 | +                          | DF000000250.4                                   |\n|                                5015 | L1PA12 5end                                      | L1                               | LINE                |                              58.9 |                        5.5e-19 | -                          | DF000000778.4                                   |\n|                                 347 | THE1D                                            | ERVL-MaLR                        | LTR                 |                              83.5 |                        7.4e-25 | -                          | DF000000820.4                                   |\n|                                 997 | THE1B                                            | ERVL-MaLR                        | LTR                 |                              51.1 |                        4.4e-15 | -                          | DF000000818.4                                   |\n|                                5318 | AluSx1                                           | Alu                              | SINE                |                              97.8 |                        2.6e-29 | +                          | DF000000048.4                                   |\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nenhancers, and OCRs in the human genome ( Fig. 4). We observed highly significant enrichment and depletion patterns across different repeat classes and region types. Small RNA genes showed the strongest enrichment in promoter regions, with scRNA (8.74-fold), snRNA (6.85-fold), RNA (6.60-fold), srpRNA (6.31-fold), and rRNA (5.02-fold) all significantly enriched ( q &lt; .001). Notably, tRNA genes displayed extreme enrichment in OCRs (13.69-fold) while showing more modest enrichment in promoters and enhancers. Major TE classes - including SINE, LINE, LTR, and DNA transposons-exhibited progressive depletion from promoters to OCRs. LINE elements decreased from near-neutral in promoters (1.00-fold) to substantial depletion in OCRs (0.34-fold), while the retrotransposon class showed extreme depletion in accessible chromatin (0.05-fold). In contrast, simple repeats and low complexity sequences were consistently enriched across all three regulatory region types, with low complexity sequences reaching 2.79-fold enrichment in OCRs. These distinct enrichment and depletion patterns suggest that RE distributions serve as informative features for distinguishing regulatory regions. This may explain why models trained to recognize RE during pretraining show improved performance on regulatory classification tasks, as the presence or absence of specific repeat classes provides a robust signal for identifying different types of functional regions.", "original_chars": 38815, "evidence_quote": "A crucial design choice is its single-nucleotide tokenization, where each base is treated as an individual token", "evidence_section_heading": "Results", "contains_evidence_quote": true, "contains_chunk_excerpt": true, "chunk_id": 4, "pages": [2], "doc_item_refs": ["#/texts/27", "#/texts/28", "#/texts/29"], "docling_graph_node_id": "InputRepresentationEvidence_f6a40fef1887bf27", "docling_graph_provenance": {"document_id": "73c20120db4438b0a4ac2b1ce9aff4df", "match": "verbatim", "chunk