./data/fulltext_screening_context_2026-07-10_docling_graph_direct_all235_clean_both_targets/fulltext_screening_input.jsonl:59:{"record_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_000031", "candidate_id": "full_2026-07-06__rec_000031", "source_record_id": "rec_000031", "source_corpus": "uncertain", "title": "CLAMP: predicting specific protein-mediated chromatin loops in diverse species with a chromatin accessibility language model.", "abstract": "Emerging DNA language models provide powerful tools to address the challenge of accurately predicting chromatin loops, fundamental structures governing 3D genome organization and gene regulation. Here we present CLAMP, which utilizes a deep language model pre-trained on broad cross-species chromatin accessibility data. CLAMP achieves superior performance compared to existing methods in predicting specific protein-mediated loops across 10 species, 18 proteins, and 24 cell types. CLAMP incorporates a novel CoVE explainer that reveals context-dependent genomic feature contributions, providing insights into the features driving predictions. CLAMP predictions effectively identify functionally significant chromatin loops and associated biological pathways.\n\nKeywords: Chromatin loops, 3D Genome, Language models, Epigenomics", "doi": "10.1186/s13059-026-03948-9", "year": "", "venue": "", "sources": [], "selected_full_text_sections": "[data_source: CLAMP: predicting specific protein-mediated chromatin loops in diverse species with a chromatin accessibility language model > Methods > Data collection and preprocessing > Pre-training dataset]\nPrevious studies have shown that incorporating multiple species dataset during DNA language model pre-training improves downstream task performance [28, 32, 36]. However, this approach is computationally intensive. De et al. [59] demonstrated that training DNA foundation model (SegmentNT) with just 5 species spanning certain evolutionary distances achieved remarkable results. To balance computational efficiency and model performance, we collected 59 ATAC-seq datasets across seven species with certain evolutionary distances (human, mouse, bovine, zebrafish, medaka and chicken) at different developmental  stages,  totaling  8,483,660  peak  regions.  These  datasets,  sourced  from Ouyang et al. [60] (Additional file 1: Fig. S12), provide potential anchor regions for chromatin loop formation, while their developmental dynamics help capture chromatin state changes.\n\n[input_representation: CLAMP: predicting specific protein-mediated chromatin loops in diverse species with a chromatin accessibility language model > Results > Overview of CLAMP]\nCLAMP is a computational framework for accurately predicting chromatin loops across diverse  species,  mediating  proteins,  and  cell  types  (Fig.  1).  CLAMP  integrates  three key genomic features: (1) deep semantic DNA sequence representations derived from pre-training  on  large-scale,  cross-species  chromatin  accessibility  data;  (2)  local  epigenetic signals at loop anchors; and (3) the genomic distance between anchors. The framework employs a pre-training and fine-tuning strategy.\n\nFig. 1 Overview of the CLAMP framework. a Data collection for model pre-training and fine-tuning. Pre-training utilized 8 million peaks from chromatin accessibility data across 7 species. Fine-tuning employed 3 million chromatin loops covering 10 species, sourced from GEO, Chromloops, and ENCODE databases. b The CLAMP pre-training phase. DNA sequences from peak regions are extracted and processed via a tokenizer to form the input corpus. The resulting pre-trained CLAMP representations are applicable to various genomic tasks. c The CLAMP fine-tuning phase. Three data modalities are integrated for chromatin loop prediction: DNA sequence, local epigenetic signals, and genomic distance. Post fine-tuning, the CoVE Explainer enables model interpretation\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nDuring pre-training (Fig. 1b), we used 59 chromatin accessibility datasets from 7 species to train a 12-layer Transformer encoder. This deep language model learns to predict masked sequence fragments (k-mers) [39], capturing DNA sequence syntax and longrange dependencies relevant to higher-order chromatin structure. This process generates informative sequence representations and establishes a foundation for subsequent task-specific fine-tuning.\n\nIn the fine-tuning phase (Fig. 1c), the pre-trained model is adapted for specific loop prediction tasks. This stage fuses multi-modal information: it not only utilizes the pretrained encoder to extract deep features from anchor DNA sequences but also concurrently processes local epigenetic signals at the anchor regions and the genomic distance between anchor pairs. To model the symmetric nature of loop interactions [40], CLAMP incorporates a symmetric multi-head attention mechanism that integrates sequence and signal  features  from  both  anchors.  These  fused  features,  along  with  the  inter-anchor genomic  distance,  are  input  into  a  classifier  that  predicts  the  probability  of  loop  formation. To enhance adaptability, reduce computational cost, and preserve pre-trained knowledge,  we  utilized  the  (IA) 3 [41]  parameter-efficient  fine-tuning  technique.  This allows  for  the  fine-tuning  of  nearly  0.066%  of  the  total  parameters,  enabling  efficient adaptation across numerous chromatin loop datasets representing 10 species, 18 protein factors, and 24 cell types.\n\nThrough  this  strategy,  which  combines  deep  sequence  pre-training,  multi-modal information fusion, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning, the CLAMP framework aims to provide a powerful and flexible tool to tackle the challenge of accurately predicting chromatin loops in diverse biological contexts.", "section_evidence": [{"section_type": "data_source", "heading": "Pre-training dataset", "heading_path": ["CLAMP: predicting specific protein-mediated chromatin loops in diverse species with a chromatin accessibility language model", "Methods", "Data collection and preprocessing", "Pre-training dataset"], "heading_path_text": "CLAMP: predicting specific protein-mediated chromatin loops in diverse species with a chromatin accessibility language model > Methods > Data collection and preprocessing > Pre-training dataset", "heading_level": 5, "line_start": 197, "line_end": 200, "text": "Previous studies have shown that incorporating multiple species dataset during DNA language model pre-training improves downstream task performance [28, 32, 36]. However, this approach is computationally intensive. De et al. [59] demonstrated that training DNA foundation model (SegmentNT) with just 5 species spanning certain evolutionary distances achieved remarkable results. To balance computational efficiency and model performance, we collected 59 ATAC-seq datasets across seven species with certain evolutionary distances (human, mouse, bovine, zebrafish, medaka and chicken) at different developmental  stages,  totaling  8,483,660  peak  regions.  These  datasets,  sourced  from Ouyang et al. [60] (Additional file 1: Fig. S12), provide potential anchor regions for chromatin loop formation, while their developmental dynamics help capture chromatin state changes.", "original_chars": 874, "evidence_quote": "we collected 59 ATAC-seq datasets across seven species", "evidence_section_heading": "Methods > Data collection and preprocessing > Pre-training dataset", "contains_evidence_quote": true, "contains_chunk_excerpt": true, "chunk_id": 18, "pages": [18], "doc_item_refs": ["#/texts/122"], "docling_graph_node_id": "DataSourceEvidence_bf146b315f7c3662", "docling_graph_provenance": {"document_id": "82deac554219f7db64c984f693faa552", "match": "verbatim", "chunks": [18], "pages": [18], "refs": ["#/texts/122"], "spans": [{"chunk": 18, "start": 639, "end": 680}]}, "grounding_source": "docling_graph_provenance", "match_type": "verbatim", "source_markdown": "data/docli