he CoVE Explainer enables model interpretation\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nDuring pre-training (Fig. 1b), we used 59 chromatin accessibility datasets from 7 species to train a 12-layer Transformer encoder. This deep language model learns to predict masked sequence fragments (k-mers) [39], capturing DNA sequence syntax and longrange dependencies relevant to higher-order chromatin structure. This process generates informative sequence representations and establishes a foundation for subsequent task-specific fine-tuning.\n\nIn the fine-tuning phase (Fig. 1c), the pre-trained model is adapted for specific loop prediction tasks. This stage fuses multi-modal information: it not only utilizes the pretrained encoder to extract deep features from anchor DNA sequences but also concurrently processes local epigenetic signals at the anchor regions and the genomic distance between anchor pairs. To model the symmetric nature of loop interactions [40], CLAMP incorporates a symmetric multi-head attention mechanism that integrates sequence and signal  features  from  both  anchors.  These  fused  features,  along  with  the  inter-anchor genomic  distance,  are  input  into  a  classifier  that  predicts  the  probability  of  loop  formation. To enhance adaptability, reduce computational cost, and preserve pre-trained knowledge,  we  utilized  the  (IA) 3 [41]  parameter-efficient  fine-tuning  technique.  This allows  for  the  fine-tuning  of  nearly  0.066%  of  the  total  parameters,  enabling  efficient adaptation across numerous chromatin loop datasets representing 10 species, 18 protein factors, and 24 cell types.\n\nThrough  this  strategy,  which  combines  deep  sequence  pre-training,  multi-modal information fusion, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning, the CLAMP framework aims to provide a powerful and flexible tool to tackle the challenge of accurately predicting chromatin loops in diverse biological contexts.", "original_chars": 3169, "ev