./data/input_representation_taxonomy_2026-07-11/runs/discovery_open_r1/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003131/full_2026_07_06_rec_003131_docling_json_20260711_005126/docling/chunks.json:1032:    "text": "3 Method\n3.1 Data\nThe datasets in genomics, proteomics, and medical genetics have experienced significant growth in both scale and diversity. Our dataset-building attempt starts from the gene entities and their textual descriptions maintained by the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) [79]. Taking the 192064 individual gene items from NCBI as the entries, we then collected and screened the data of related bio-factors from multiple datasets. Each of these datasets stands for a particular level in the genomics system(Fig. 1).\nFigure 1: Integration of multiple datasets.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nComposite scientific figure showing a training corpus overview. Left side depicts biomedical data sources/logos feeding a central rounded box labeled “Training Corpus,” including OMIM, NCBI Gene, UniProt, dbGaP, and DisGeNET. Right side is a horizontal bar chart comparing corpus/source contents.\n\nFigure type: workflow/data-source schematic plus horizontal bar plot.\n\nVisible labels/legend: sources include “PcodeG(Human),” “NCBI,” “DisGeNet,” “OMIM,” “Uniport,” and “dbGaP.” Legend categories include Pheno/Disease, Protein, Gene, Pairs(GPheno), and Pairs(ProG).\n\nMeasured quantities: counts of biomedical entities and entity pairs, including genes, proteins, phenotypes/diseases, gene-phenotype pairs, and protein-gene pairs.\n\nMain visible finding: NCBI and dbGaP show the largest pair counts, with annotated values around 192,064 and 136,526 respectively; DisGeNET has a large count around 99,214. PcodeG(Human) is dominated by gene counts.",
